  "final_text": "## Result\n\nAt W = 1.5929 GeV, Q\u00b2 = 0.4276 GeV\u00b2, cos \u03b8* = -0.527586, \u03c6* = 300\u00b0 (CLAS12 defaults: model=3 EtaMAID, rc_mode=0 full O(\u03b1), bmom=6.53 GeV, ivec=1, vcut=0.166 GeV\u00b2):\n\n- **\u03b4 (\u03c3_obs/\u03c3_Born)** = 0.9509747313\n- **\u03c3_Born** = 0.0071018161 (unpolarized 5-fold differential Born cross section, code's native units)\n\nBoth are exact values from `eta/radtot.dat`. This point is above the ep\u2192e'p\u03b7 threshold (W_min = m_p+m_\u03b7 = 1.4861 GeV) and within the tabulated grid, so the calculation is physical \u2014 the run completed cleanly with no NaNs, integration errors, or unphysical-point flags.\n\nOne gotcha worth flagging: the prebuilt `exclurad` binary hard-codes `open(unit=5,file='input.dat')` in the Fortran source, so `./exclurad < input.dat` silently ignores stdin redirection and just reads whatever `input.dat` already contains in the working directory. I had to overwrite `eta/input.dat` itself with the requested kinematics before running. Details and full column mapping are in `outcome.json`.",
